ROLE
You are a senior full-stack engineer. Build a complete, production-ready multiplayer quiz game. The title is a placeholder: make it one config constant, GAME_NAME. Work in phases and run tests after each one. Do not stop to ask questions: where something is unspecified, choose the simplest sensible option and record it in ASSUMPTIONS.md.

STACK
TypeScript everywhere. React (Vite) frontend, Node.js + Socket.io server, Supabase (Postgres) for persistence. Monorepo: packages/engine (pure game logic, zero I/O), apps/server, apps/web. Mobile-first UI, because most players will be on phones.

THE GAME (implement these rules exactly)
Players climb 60 levels by answering questions. Each level has one MAIN question and up to 4 ROOM questions, each room easier than the one before.

Player state: { level: 0..60 (highest level cleared, 0 = start), phase: MAIN | ROOM, room: 0..4 (0 while in MAIN), resets, finished }. The MAIN question being asked is always for level + 1.

Transitions:
1. MAIN correct: level += 1 and serve the next MAIN question. When level reaches 60, the player finishes.
2. MAIN wrong: go to ROOM 1 (easier question).
3. ROOM k wrong, k < 4: go to ROOM k+1 (easier than ROOM k).
4. ROOM 4 wrong: RESET. level = 0, phase = MAIN, resets += 1. This is 5 consecutive wrong answers (main + all 4 rooms).
5. ROOM k correct, any k: return to MAIN for the same level. The player must answer MAIN to progress, and it is a different variant question of the same difficulty.
6. Timer expiry counts as a wrong answer.
There are no checkpoints and no other penalties.

DIFFICULTY AND SELECTION
Every question has difficulty 1-60. MAIN at level L uses difficulty L. ROOM k uses max(1, round(L * roomDifficultyFractions[k-1])), default [0.8, 0.6, 0.4, 0.2]. At low levels this floors at 1, so still serve a different question. Selection rules:
- Pick the exact difficulty, else the nearest available.
- Never repeat a question for the same player within a match unless the pool is exhausted.
- Randomize per player, so neighbours never see the same question at the same moment.
- Shuffle option order on every delivery.

CONFIG (one typed object, host-overridable per match; defaults)
totalLevels 60; roomsPerLevel 4; roomDifficultyFractions as above; mainTimerSec 45; roomTimerSec 30; maxMainFailsPerLevel null (optional anti-stall: when set, that many failed MAIN attempts on the same level force a reset; off by default); matchDurationMinutes null (optional: when set, the match ends at timeout).

QUESTIONS
Schema: id, type (default "mcq"; leave room for numeric and true/false), text, options[4], correct_option, difficulty 1-60, topic, explanation (optional), active. Ship seed.json with at least 120 placeholder CS questions, 2 or more per difficulty step. The host UI imports CSV/JSON and shows a validation report plus a coverage heatmap by difficulty, warning below 4 questions per difficulty.

REAL-TIME (server-authoritative)
Clients never see correct answers and never decide outcomes. The server sends each question with a one-time questionToken and a server-side deadline. The client submits {questionToken, optionId}. The server validates the token, the deadline, and one answer per token.
Events: match:join, match:state, question:show, answer:submit, answer:result {correct, transition: ADVANCE | ENTER_ROOM | NEXT_ROOM | RETURN_TO_MAIN | RESET | FINISH}, players:update (every player's level, phase, room), match:ended.
Reconnecting restores the player's state and current question with the remaining time. Rate-limit submissions and ignore duplicates.

PERSISTENCE (Supabase)
Tables: matches (id, code, status, config, started_at, ends_at), players (id, match_id, name, level, phase, room, resets, best_level, finished_at), questions, attempts (player_id, question_id, phase, room, level, correct, time_ms, created_at). Players are anonymous: they join with a 6-character match code plus a display name. RLS: clients can never read questions.correct_option. Only the server (service role) touches it.

SCREENS
Player:
- Join screen, then the play screen.
- A 0-60 progress track and a phase banner: MAIN, or "ROOM 3/4" with the background getting darker the deeper the room.
- Timer ring, question card, and four large tap targets.
- Outcome animations: rise (ADVANCE), drop (ENTER_ROOM / NEXT_ROOM), climb back (RETURN_TO_MAIN), collapse (RESET), celebration (FINISH).
- A compact live panel of other players showing name, level, and depth pips for how deep in the rooms they are. Anyone in ROOM 4 is highlighted as "one mistake from reset".
- Cosmetic theme change every 10 levels.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion, and keep sound off by default with a toggle.
Host:
- Create a match and get its code, and configure the rules.
- Upload and validate questions.
- Start, pause, and end the match.
- A live dashboard, remove-player action, and results CSV export.
Projector:
- A big-screen leaderboard plus a live "danger zone" list, auto-updating.

RANKING
Current level descending, ties broken by who reached it earlier. Also show best_level, resets, and accuracy. Untimed matches: first to clear level 60 wins and the rest are ranked by current level. Timed matches: rank by the rule above when time ends.

ANTI-CHEAT
Per-player question order and option shuffle, server-only timers, no answers in any payload, log tab-visibility changes (flag them, don't punish), and one active session per player.

TESTING (required)
Table-driven unit tests for the engine covering every transition above, plus:
- A ROOM 4 correct answer returns to MAIN.
- Clearing level 60 finishes the player.
- A timeout counts as wrong.
- Room difficulty is non-increasing across rooms and floors at 1.
- maxMainFailsPerLevel behaves correctly when set.
- A player never gets a repeated question.
Also write scripts/simulate.ts: it simulates N bots with a configurable accuracy-versus-difficulty curve and prints the level distribution, reset frequency, and average time to finish, so the host can tune the fractions and timers.

BUILD ORDER
1. Engine, tests, and simulator
2. Question selector, seed data, and validator
3. Server, Socket.io, and Supabase
4. Player UI
5. Host UI
6. Projector view
7. Polish, README (setup, env vars, deploy), and ASSUMPTIONS.md
After each phase, run the tests and summarize in 3 lines.

DEFINITION OF DONE
Ten simulated bots and three real browser clients complete a full match without desync. Every transition in the rules is covered by a passing test, and no client payload ever contains a correct answer.